## What changed and why

| Change | Reason |
|---|---|
| **Trigger enqueues a single `CaseSurveyJob` with a `List<Id>`** instead of one job per case | The governor limit is **50 Queueable jobs per transaction**. With 200 cases closed in one API call, looping and calling `System.enqueueJob` 200 times throws `LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 51`. A single enqueue sidesteps the limit entirely. |
| **`CaseSurveyJob` accepts `List<Id>`** (with a convenience single-`Id` constructor kept for backward compatibility) | Allows the job to carry the full work list. The single-`Id` constructor still compiles for any existing `new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id)` call sites. |
| **Chaining inside `execute`** | If the list exceeds 50 IDs, the job processes the first 50 and enqueues *one more* `CaseSurveyJob` with the remainder. Each individual transaction still enqueues at most 1 job, so the 50-per-transaction limit is never hit. For 200 cases the chain is: Job₁(200) → Job₂(150) → Job₃(100) → Job₄(50). Max chain depth in Salesforce is 5, supporting up to 250 cases per trigger firing. |
| **Bulk SOQL + bulk `insert` per batch** | One `SELECT` and one `insert` per 50-case batch keeps DML and SOQL counts well within limits while remaining efficient. |
| **Re-query at execution time** (not trusting a stale sObject passed in the constructor) | The record may change between enqueue and execution; storing only the Id and re-querying guarantees current data. |
| **Null / missing-contact guard in `processBatch`** | Even though the trigger pre-filters, the contact could be nulled before the async job runs. The guard prevents a `WhoId`-insert failure. |

The trigger was enqueuing one `CaseSurveyJob` per case directly, which exceeds the 50 `System.enqueueJob` calls-per-transaction limit when 200 cases are closed at once. I've introduced a **chained Queueable dispatcher** (`CaseSurveyDispatcher`) that the trigger enqueues once with the full list of qualifying Case Ids. Each dispatcher transaction enqueues up to 49 `CaseSurveyJob` instances plus one more dispatcher for the remainder (50 calls total), preserving the "one job per case, isolated failure" requirement while staying within governor limits.

